rounding down to the nearest whole number.\n     \\[\n     \\frac{200}{7} \\approx 28.5714 \\implies \\lfloor 28.5714 \\rfloor = 28\n     \\]\n     So, the largest multiple of 7 is \\( 7 \\times 28 = 196 \\).\n\n2. **List the multiples of 7 between 100 and 200:**\n   The multiples of 7 between 105 and 196 are:\n   \\[\n   105, 112, 119, \\ldots, 196\n   \\]\n   This is an arithmetic sequence where the first term \\( a = 105 \\) and the common difference \\( d = 7 \\).\n\n3. **Determine the number of terms in the sequence:**\n   The number of terms \\( n \\) in the sequence can be found using the formula for the \\( n \\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence:\n   \\[\n   a_n = a + (n-1)d\n   \\]\n   Setting \\( a_n = 196 \\):\n   \\[\n   196 = 105 + (n-1) \\times 7\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( n \\):\n   \\[\n   196 - 105 = (n-1) \\times 7 \\implies 91 = (n-1) \\times 7 \\implies n-1 = 13 \\implies n = 14\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the sum of the arithmetic sequence:**\n   The sum \\( S_n \\) of the first \\( n \\) terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n   \\[\n   S_n = \\frac{n}{2} (a + l)\n   \\]\n   where \\( l \\) is the last term. Here, \\( n = 14 \\), \\( a = 105 \\), and \\( l = 196 \\):\n   \\[\n   S_{14} = \\frac{14}{2} (105 + 196) = 7 \\times 201 = 1407\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of all multiples of 7 between 100 and 200 is \\(\\boxed{1407}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1407}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:748"}
